// llmrun: a language model, from a GGUF file, running on the GPU. It loads a Qwen3 (or a Llama-layout model: the
// two layouts are the same but for Qwen3's per-head RMSNorm of the queries and keys and its rotation order),
// builds the tokenizer from the file's metadata, runs tokens through the layers on the GPU with a KV cache, and
// chooses the next token (greedy, or temperature with top-k and top-p). It is a library: a Cone program calls it in
// its own process. No HTTP, no server. examples/llmchat.cone is a chat program over it; examples/llmcheck.cone prints
// every step's largest logits and the speed, and is what the model is compared with llama.cpp by.
//
//   imm modelFile = Path.from("C:/src/llm-models/Qwen3-8B-Q4_K_M.gguf");
//   var instance = Instance.createHeadless("chat", false);        // no window, no validation layer (Instance.create has both)
//   var none = Surface.none();
//   var adapter = instance.requestAdapter(&none, POWER_PREFERENCE_HIGH_PERFORMANCE);
//   var device = adapter.requestComputeDevice(&ComputeRequest(largeBuffers: true));    // the weights bind over 128 MiB
//   match Engine.open(&mut device, &modelFile, &LoadOptions(4096usize, 64usize)) {    // 4096 positions, 64-token chunks
//     case var Ok(engine) {
//       match loadTokens(engine.ggufModel()) {
//         case let Ok(vocab) {
//           imm turn = vocab.userTurn("Why is the sky blue?", false);                  // ChatML; false: no thinking
//           mut logits = engine.feed(&mut device, turn.view(), true) ...               // the last token's logits
//           imm sampler = TokenSampler.make(0.7, 20usize, 0.8);
//           mut rng = Rng.fromSeed(1u64);
//           imm id = sampler.sample(logits.view(), &mut rng);                          // the first token of the reply
//           if vocab.isStop(id) ...  else  engine.feed(&mut device, [id], true) ...    // and so on
//
// examples/llmchat.cone is that loop, whole, with streaming, the end of a turn and tokens a second.
//
//   Engine.open(&mut device, &path, &LoadOptions(ctx, chunk))  Result[Engine, String]: why not, in words
//   engine.feed(&mut device, tokens, wantLogits)               run tokens after the ones already run; the last one's logits
//   engine.pos  engine.ctx  engine.reset()  engine.rewind(p)   the cache's place and room
//   engine.shape()  engine.info  engine.ggufModel()            what the file said
//   planModel(&path, &options, bindingSize, deviceLocal)       what loading would take, allocating nothing
//   loadTokens(model)  ModelTokens: tok, stops, isStop, text, userTurn, systemTurn, closeTurn
//   TextStream.make()  stream.push(&tok, id, showSpecial)      the bytes of the tokens so far that are whole characters
//   TokenSampler.greedy()  TokenSampler.make(temp, topK, topP)  sampler.sample(logits, &mut rng)   argmax  topCandidates
//   syntheticGguf(&weights, "qwen3")  syntheticGgufWith(..., &Flaws)   a random model as a GGUF file (tests; llmreference)
//   utf8Console  argValue  hasFlag  parseUInt  parseFloat  readLine  writeBytes  oneDecimal    for a terminal program
//
// WHAT IS RUN. Architectures `qwen3` and `llama`, read from `general.architecture`, with every size from the file's
// metadata (`<arch>.block_count`, `.embedding_length`, `.feed_forward_length`, `.attention.head_count`,
// `.head_count_kv`, `.key_length`, `.layer_norm_rms_epsilon`, `.rope.freq_base`, `.rope.dimension_count` when
// present, `.context_length`), tied or separate output, any of GGUF's F32, F16, Q8_0, Q4_K and Q6_K weights. A file
// that has anything else is refused, in words, before anything is allocated: another architecture, a model that
// scales its rotary embedding, a tensor of another type (Q4_0, Q5_K, ...) or one that is missing, a context longer
// than the model was trained for, or one the GPU's memory cannot hold (with the largest that would fit).
//
// THE GPU'S MEMORY. Weights go in a few buffers of at most 1 GiB (a matrix never straddles two: a kernel's byte
// offset is 32 bits), in file order; each norm vector is a buffer of its own; each layer's K and V cache is a buffer of
// ctx * kvDim f32s; the scratch is sized for one chunk of tokens. The token table stays in the mapped file when the
// output is untied (a token's row is decoded on the CPU and uploaded, 8 KB for the 8B), and is uploaded only when the
// output head is the table itself. The budget at a context is weights + KV cache (288 KiB a position for the 8B, 224
// KiB for the 1.7B) + scratch, and 512 MiB are kept for the system. Qwen3-8B Q4_K_M: 4.35 GiB of weights in 5
// buffers, 5.5 GiB at 4096 positions, 6.7 GiB at 8192 (of the 4060's 7.77 GiB). Qwen3-1.7B Q8_0: 1.7 GiB in 2,
// 2.6 GiB at 4096.
//
// THE BUFFERS ARE RESIDENT; A STEP IS ONE JOB. A gpuwork Job owns the buffers given to it and hands them back with its
// Completion, so each call of 'feed' (for a chunk of up to `chunk` tokens) runs one job over all the engine's buffers
// (in a fixed order that is their slot), records the layers' dispatches, reads back the logits and gets the buffers
// back in the Completion. A Qwen3 layer is 20 dispatches (attention norm; q, k, v; q norm, k norm; two rotations; two
// appends to the cache; attention; output projection; residual add; FFN norm; gate, up; SiLU; multiply; down;
// residual add) and a 36-layer decode step is 722. The first job is given the buffers; each later one is made from the
// last job's Completion ('completion.again'), which moves the same buffers back into the same slots and passes on the
// job's bind groups, so a step makes none of its own: a dispatch whose kernel and bindings are those of the same place
// in the job before uses that job's bind group. A dispatch's parameters (the kernel's small by-value struct) are not
// a buffer each but a 256-byte-aligned range of one parameter slab the job holds ('job.slab', 'job.pack'), written
// with one upload when the job is submitted, so a step's only per-step CPU-to-GPU writes are the token's embedding,
// the slab (the position among its fields) and the logits' readback. A prompt chunk of two or more tokens uses the
// matrix-matrix kernel where a single token uses the matrix-vector one, so the first decode step after a prompt
// makes the bind groups of those dispatches again; every later step reuses all.
//
// METHOD CHOICES. These are defaults a later design may revisit. Each says what was chosen and what was left out.
//
// - CORRECTNESS FIRST, THEN THE DISPATCH COST, THEN THE KERNELS. What a decode step costs and where the time goes is in
//   the measurements below. The first version made a buffer and a bind group for each of a step's 722 dispatches and
//   freed them again; the parameter slab, the bind groups kept from step to step and one compute pass for a job
//   took that away (gpuwork's header says how). What is left of a decode step is the kernels' own time.
// - THE CPU REFERENCE FIRST. llmreference's forwardCpu is the model on the CPU over the existing twins. tests/forward.cone
//   writes four small random models as GGUF files (Qwen3 with the Q4_K_M mix and untied output, Qwen3 in Q8_0 with the
//   output tied to the table, a Llama with F16 and F32 weights and the other rotation order and no q/k norm, a query
//   wider than the model), loads them as a downloaded file is loaded, and checks every logit of a prompt and of three
//   more tokens, one at a time, against the CPU's. Its tolerance and what it is derived from are in its header.
// - GREEDY IS THE ARGMAX WITH THE LOWEST ID AMONG EQUALS (what llama.cpp's greedy sampler does). With a temperature,
//   llama.cpp's order: the top-k logits (k = 0 means the 1000 largest), the smallest set of those whose
//   probabilities at temperature 1 reach top-p, then the temperature over that set and one draw from the
//   generator. (Hugging Face applies the temperature first; the two differ only for 0 < p < 1 with a temperature
//   other than 1.)
// - THE TOKENIZER is bpetokenize's, built from `tokenizer.ggml.tokens`, `.merges`, `.pre` and `.token_type` (types 3
//   and 4, control and user defined, are the special tokens, what llama.cpp matches in a text). The chat template is
//   ChatML, written in Cone, not the file's Jinja: a user's text is encoded as text (a typed `<|im_end|>` stays
//   text); thinking off adds Qwen3's own empty `<think>\n\n</think>\n\n`; a reply that ended at `<|im_end|>` is closed
//   (the token and a newline are run) before the next turn, so the cache holds what the template's text holds.
//   tests/tokenizer.cone checks 28 varied texts against llama-tokenize.
// - STOP TOKENS are the file's eos_token_id and the ChatML end of turn and end of text, found by their text.
// - THE EMBEDDING IS LOOKED UP ON THE CPU (gguf's rowToF32 on the mapped file) and written into a buffer before the
//   job: no kernel for it.
// - Left out: rope scaling, sliding windows, a Q4_0/Q5/IQ kernel, F16 or quantised KV caches, several sequences at
//   once, prefix reuse across sequences, sampling penalties, a stop-string, a server.
//
// CHECKED AGAINST LLAMA.CPP (b11748, its Vulkan build on the same GPU; examples/llmcheck.cone against llama-server
// with the prompt given as token ids, temperature 0, 64 tokens): Qwen3-1.7B Q8_0, 3 of 3 prompts identical;
// Qwen3-8B Q4_K_M, 4 of 6 identical and the other 2 diverge at a near-tie (the two candidates 0.0004 and 0.03 logits
// apart on this side, 0.005 and 0.04 on llama.cpp's). The top-5 logit gaps agree to 0.03 to 0.4: llama.cpp's
// kernels quantise the activations to 8 bits, these keep them in f32.
//
// MEASURED (RTX 4060 Laptop, release build, examples/llmcheck.cone; a decode step is recording the job on the CPU,
// waiting for the GPU, cleanup after). Before the change (the first version: a buffer and a bind group for each of
// the 722 dispatches of a 36-layer step, made and freed again, in a program with the Vulkan validation layer on, as
// `Instance.create` makes it where the SDK is installed): Qwen3-1.7B Q8_0 decoded 3.7 to 3.9 tokens a second
// (84 to 93 ms recording, 83 ms waiting for the GPU, 90 ms cleanup), Qwen3-8B Q4_K_M 2.4 (111, 186, 120 ms). Now,
// with the slab, kept bind groups and one pass, in a program with no validation layer (examples/llmchat.cone
// and llmcheck.cone make `Instance.createHeadless(name, false)` unless given --validate): the 1.7B decodes 22.1 to
// 22.5 tokens a second, a step 2 ms recording, 42 ms on the GPU, 0.4 ms cleanup; with --validate 18.3 to 19.4 (8 to 10 ms recording, the layer's cost). The 8B, taken with the GPU
// to itself and the layer on: 5.7 tokens a second, 14 ms recording, 160 ms on the GPU, 0.4 ms cleanup. What is left
// is the kernels: the 8B's 160 ms is 4.35 GiB of weights read at about 29 GB/s on a card rated 256 (llmkernels).
// Prefill, a 231-token prompt in chunks of 64: 125 tokens a second before, 208 to 214 now on the 1.7B, which is the
// matrix-matrix kernel's time (llmkernels' measured ~700 GFLOP/s); a prompt of 5 tokens 17 before, 45 now; of 11,
// 38 before, 98 now (the fixed cost of a job fell from about 290 ms to 110). The
// first decode step after a prompt makes the bind groups of its matrix-vector dispatches again (the prompt used the
// matrix-matrix kernel), the later ones none: llmcheck prints how many were reused.
//
// The tests: tests/forward.cone (GPU against the CPU reference; a GPU), tests/generate.cone (a small random model with
// the real vocabulary, end to end; a GPU), tests/plan.cone (the header-only plan, the refusals), tests/tokenizer.cone
// (against llama.cpp's ids), tests/sampler.cone. The tests that need a model file print a SKIPPED line and run
// none of their checks (their output then differs from the expected) when `C:\src\llm-models\Qwen3-*.gguf` is absent. LLMRUN_REPORT=1 prints the numbers the tests derive their bounds from.

mod llmrun;

import libc;
import collections use List, String;
import time use Instant;
import path use Path;
import random use Rng;
import fs;
import gguf;
import bpetokenize;
import gpu use *;
import gpuwork use *;
import llmreference use *;
import llmkernels use *;
